# The State ledger is append-only and written from every machine that shares the vault: git
# unions the lines instead of stopping the rebase, and the fold is order-independent (latest
# valid_from wins), so a union is always the right merge for it.
State/facts.jsonl merge=union
State/proposed.jsonl merge=union
# Deliberately NOT Daily/*.md. A union merge of two machines' renders of the same day keeps BOTH
# briefing blocks, silently; a stopped rebase is loud. With the pull step on, the briefing is
# never rendered on a stale tree, so a daily-note conflict should not arise — and if it does, a
# human should see it.
# Line endings for what an interpreter reads. With core.autocrlf=true (the Git for Windows
# installer default) the hook was checked out with CRLF: '#!/bin/sh\r' does not run and sh
# rejects every line, so every commit — the nightly backup's too — was refused.
# These rules reach fresh checkouts only: a vault that already has the CRLF hook keeps it
# (the blob is unchanged, so git never rewrites the file and the tree looks clean). Repair it
# once with `python tools/setup.py --fix-line-endings`; every setup.py run also does it.
tools/githooks/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
